WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.750 --> 00:00:01.590
- [Narrator] From circuit boards

00:00:01.590 --> 00:00:04.870
and electronic components that
end up in some Apple products

00:00:04.870 --> 00:00:07.610
to patio furniture and
plenty of other things.

00:00:07.610 --> 00:00:10.900
A lot of the goods we use every
day are produced in China.

00:00:10.900 --> 00:00:12.810
And those factories have
been getting messages

00:00:12.810 --> 00:00:14.090
from the local government

00:00:14.090 --> 00:00:16.070
that there's not enough electricity.

00:00:16.070 --> 00:00:18.260
And a lot of cities have had blackouts.

00:00:18.260 --> 00:00:21.000
- Restrictions came quite unexpected,

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:23.600
we're allowed to work
during restricted hours,

00:00:23.600 --> 00:00:25.630
which was mostly at night.

00:00:25.630 --> 00:00:26.890
- [Narrator] Right now, China is facing

00:00:26.890 --> 00:00:28.150
its biggest power crisis.

00:00:28.150 --> 00:00:29.130
And more than a decade,

00:00:29.130 --> 00:00:31.100
which is leading to power outages

00:00:31.100 --> 00:00:32.590
and temporary factory closures

00:00:32.590 --> 00:00:35.700
in one of the world's
largest manufacturing hubs.

00:00:35.700 --> 00:00:37.930
- If Chinese factories
continue having problems

00:00:37.930 --> 00:00:39.080
getting electricity,

00:00:39.080 --> 00:00:41.400
there could be ripple
effects around the world.

00:00:41.400 --> 00:00:43.143
And the main problem is coal.

00:00:44.310 --> 00:00:46.030
China is the world's biggest consumer

00:00:46.030 --> 00:00:47.620
of the dirty fossil fuel

00:00:47.620 --> 00:00:50.340
and its dependency on it is
exposing some pain points

00:00:50.340 --> 00:00:52.920
in the countries transitioned
to a greener feature

00:00:52.920 --> 00:00:55.580
while threatening to delay how
quickly we'll be able to get

00:00:55.580 --> 00:00:57.273
our made in China products.

00:00:58.890 --> 00:01:00.560
To keep manufacturing hubs running

00:01:00.560 --> 00:01:02.240
and the lights on for citizens,

00:01:02.240 --> 00:01:04.070
China needs a lot of power.

00:01:04.070 --> 00:01:07.740
And last year, about 60% of
it came from burning coal.

00:01:07.740 --> 00:01:10.010
But recently this demand has soared.

00:01:10.010 --> 00:01:12.380
- China recovered from the economic impact

00:01:12.380 --> 00:01:15.340
of the COVID-19 pandemic much
earlier than other economies.

00:01:15.340 --> 00:01:17.230
So as a result, it has been producing

00:01:17.230 --> 00:01:18.670
and exporting more goods,

00:01:18.670 --> 00:01:21.363
which require a lot
more electricity usage.

00:01:22.210 --> 00:01:24.140
- [Narrator] It's factories
ramped up to make components

00:01:24.140 --> 00:01:25.980
for I-phones and video game gadgets

00:01:25.980 --> 00:01:28.680
that consumers in the west
wanted while stuck at home.

00:01:29.840 --> 00:01:31.900
But China hasn't been
able to source enough coal

00:01:31.900 --> 00:01:34.790
from domestic producers because
there's been safety issues

00:01:34.790 --> 00:01:37.840
in coal mines and anti-corruption probes.

00:01:37.840 --> 00:01:40.600
Plus, flooding in one of
China's coal mining provinces

00:01:40.600 --> 00:01:42.720
is also threatening the supply.

00:01:42.720 --> 00:01:45.410
At the same time, importing
hasn't been easy either.

00:01:45.410 --> 00:01:47.400
- They have imposed this unofficial ban

00:01:47.400 --> 00:01:49.340
on Australian coal imports.

00:01:49.340 --> 00:01:53.580
One of the usual sources that
they would go to for coal.

00:01:53.580 --> 00:01:54.600
- [Narrator] Since late last year,

00:01:54.600 --> 00:01:56.900
China has barred coal from Australia.

00:01:56.900 --> 00:01:59.560
After Canberra called for an
independent global inquiry

00:01:59.560 --> 00:02:01.813
into the origins of COVID-19.

00:02:02.690 --> 00:02:04.720
So China has had to find other suppliers

00:02:04.720 --> 00:02:06.550
in Indonesia and Mongolia.

00:02:06.550 --> 00:02:08.700
But sourcing has been
getting more difficult

00:02:08.700 --> 00:02:10.210
because now other parts of the world

00:02:10.210 --> 00:02:12.640
are beginning to emerge
from the pandemic too.

00:02:12.640 --> 00:02:14.750
And there's not enough coal to go around.

00:02:14.750 --> 00:02:17.410
- So, right now, coal prices have climbed

00:02:17.410 --> 00:02:19.650
to their highest level in a decade.

00:02:19.650 --> 00:02:21.820
And so when coal prices climb higher,

00:02:21.820 --> 00:02:25.100
it really disincentivizes
power plants in China

00:02:25.100 --> 00:02:28.030
from generating more power,
even if the demand is there.

00:02:28.030 --> 00:02:29.840
- That's because until very recently,

00:02:29.840 --> 00:02:31.900
government rules prevented power producers

00:02:31.900 --> 00:02:34.490
from passing on higher costs to customers.

00:02:34.490 --> 00:02:36.320
So instead of firing up the generators,

00:02:36.320 --> 00:02:38.492
they've chosen to cut down production.

00:02:38.492 --> 00:02:42.181
All this has led to widespread
power shortages in China.

00:02:42.181 --> 00:02:46.310
(speaking in foreign language)

00:02:46.310 --> 00:02:48.360
It's also effected factories like this one

00:02:48.360 --> 00:02:50.990
in the Southern manufacturing
hub of Guangdong.

00:02:50.990 --> 00:02:55.670
- We'd have to adapt ourselves
to these restrictions.

00:02:55.670 --> 00:02:57.110
- [Narrator] Thomas Broertjes's manages

00:02:57.110 --> 00:02:58.863
this patio furniture factory.

00:03:00.620 --> 00:03:01.950
He says since early August,

00:03:01.950 --> 00:03:04.480
he's been receiving text messages
from the local government

00:03:04.480 --> 00:03:06.770
asking to restrict power usage.

00:03:06.770 --> 00:03:08.510
- They send daily message.

00:03:08.510 --> 00:03:11.750
They give a specific period
where you are allowed

00:03:11.750 --> 00:03:14.060
to work in and yeah,

00:03:14.060 --> 00:03:17.683
then we will have to adjust
our production schedule.

00:03:19.090 --> 00:03:21.040
- [Narrator] So his factory
had to occasionally close

00:03:21.040 --> 00:03:24.830
during peak hours and open
between 11:00 PM and 8:00 AM,

00:03:24.830 --> 00:03:26.720
according to the
government's instructions.

00:03:26.720 --> 00:03:28.300
- We didn't have restrictions

00:03:28.300 --> 00:03:31.730
like that you cannot totally work anymore.

00:03:31.730 --> 00:03:36.400
So that we were still be able
to continue our production.

00:03:36.400 --> 00:03:38.970
- But several other companies
haven't been so lucky,

00:03:38.970 --> 00:03:41.400
including several Apple suppliers,

00:03:41.400 --> 00:03:43.880
Concraft, an electronic
component supplier,

00:03:43.880 --> 00:03:46.170
said it had to temporarily
suspend production

00:03:46.170 --> 00:03:47.890
for about five days.

00:03:47.890 --> 00:03:50.800
Eson precision which
supplies mechanical parts,

00:03:50.800 --> 00:03:53.760
and Unimicron technology which
makes printed circuit boards,

00:03:53.760 --> 00:03:56.720
also stopped production
for about five days.

00:03:56.720 --> 00:03:59.610
Apple didn't respond to
a request for comment.

00:03:59.610 --> 00:04:01.880
Meanwhile, more than 10
chip related companies

00:04:01.880 --> 00:04:03.530
in China's Eastern city of Cochin,

00:04:03.530 --> 00:04:06.180
said they temporarily
closed their facilities

00:04:06.180 --> 00:04:08.563
for about five days at
the end of September.

00:04:10.010 --> 00:04:12.050
Broertjes says he
continues to get messages

00:04:12.050 --> 00:04:13.770
from the government
about whether the factory

00:04:13.770 --> 00:04:15.143
can use power normally.

00:04:15.990 --> 00:04:18.070
For now, production is back on schedule,

00:04:18.070 --> 00:04:21.060
but he says he's prepared to
shut down at a moment's notice.

00:04:21.060 --> 00:04:22.780
- The chance is always there.

00:04:22.780 --> 00:04:24.230
I have learned that in China,

00:04:24.230 --> 00:04:27.800
you have to cooperate with
a certain flexibility.

00:04:27.800 --> 00:04:30.470
The rules are set, it is what it is.

00:04:30.470 --> 00:04:32.253
That's what we have to do.

00:04:32.253 --> 00:04:34.720
- [Narrator] And the
Cooperation will be important

00:04:34.720 --> 00:04:37.685
because Beijing has set
itself a big climate goal.

00:04:37.685 --> 00:04:41.602
(speaking in foreign language)

00:04:48.270 --> 00:04:50.090
- [Narrator] To make that
happen, China has been using

00:04:50.090 --> 00:04:52.750
a target, electricity use must grow

00:04:52.750 --> 00:04:54.900
at a lower rate than GDP,

00:04:54.900 --> 00:04:56.660
but in the first half of this year,

00:04:56.660 --> 00:04:59.620
electricity use rose more than GDP

00:04:59.620 --> 00:05:02.480
and hitting Beijing's target
could become more difficult

00:05:02.480 --> 00:05:05.410
as winter approaches and
demands for heating increase.

00:05:05.410 --> 00:05:08.610
- These power outages really
show how reliant China

00:05:08.610 --> 00:05:12.260
is still on coal, despite
their push to transition

00:05:12.260 --> 00:05:16.420
into greener energy sources
and curb carbon emissions.

00:05:16.420 --> 00:05:17.820
And so going into the future,

00:05:17.820 --> 00:05:21.150
they're going to be forced
to balance some goals.

00:05:21.150 --> 00:05:22.810
- [Narrator] To balance
these goals, Beijing hinted

00:05:22.810 --> 00:05:25.310
in mid-October that it would
need to rethink its path

00:05:25.310 --> 00:05:27.970
and pacing for reaching
its climate target.

00:05:27.970 --> 00:05:30.060
China's leadership has
said electricity curves

00:05:30.060 --> 00:05:31.370
for high energy users,

00:05:31.370 --> 00:05:34.290
such as steel and cement
will continue to stay.

00:05:34.290 --> 00:05:35.660
But to address immediate needs,

00:05:35.660 --> 00:05:38.090
the country has now stepped
up its coal supplies

00:05:38.090 --> 00:05:41.363
while allowing prices of
coal fired power to increase.

00:05:42.890 --> 00:05:44.260
With all the unpredictability,

00:05:44.260 --> 00:05:46.130
factory owners like Broertjes are starting

00:05:46.130 --> 00:05:49.050
to think about how to ensure
their own stable power source.

00:05:49.050 --> 00:05:53.030
- We are also considering
about the sustainable energy,

00:05:53.030 --> 00:05:55.560
can we plant solar panels on our roof?

00:05:55.560 --> 00:05:58.910
Is there are no alternative
way of getting energy,

00:05:58.910 --> 00:06:02.190
and besides a diesel generator?

00:06:02.190 --> 00:06:03.840
- [Narrator] But, if in the
short run, the power crisis

00:06:03.840 --> 00:06:05.110
in China continues.

00:06:05.110 --> 00:06:06.980
The impact could hit closer to home.

00:06:06.980 --> 00:06:09.490
- That could weigh on
manufacturing activity

00:06:09.490 --> 00:06:10.750
and economic growth in China,

00:06:10.750 --> 00:06:13.270
which subsequently could
affect the rest of the world.

00:06:13.270 --> 00:06:15.340
Whether people are going to
be able to get their products

00:06:15.340 --> 00:06:18.983
on time or we'll even face
higher costs for them.

